 to be released under the GPL, source included. That conflicts directly with shipping closed source. Distribution includes shipping binaries, installers, desktop or mobile apps, and on-prem or appliance deliveries. If you only run it on your own servers as a hosted service, GPL-3.0 usually isn't triggered. AGPL would be, but this one is plain GPL.\n- **requests (Apache-2.0):** This is fine. Include the license text and any NOTICE file in your attribution, and keep the copyright notices.\n- **left-pad (MIT):** This is fine. Include the copyright and license notice.\n\n**Before you ship, you can:**\n1. Replace somelib with a permissively licensed alternative, or write the functionality yourself.\n2. Ask the somelib authors for a commercial or dual license.\n3. Isolate it behind a separate process boundary (for example, a standalone command-line tool you call). This is legally gray, so get counsel's opinion first.\n4. Confirm you're only offering it as a hosted service, if that's true, and still have counsel sign off.\n\n**Other things to check:**\n- `deps.txt` lists only direct dependencies. Transitive dependencies can bring in other licenses, so run a full scan of the resolved tree (for example `pip-licenses` or `license-checker`).\n- Check that the license labels are accurate. I'm going by what the file says, not by verifying against the packages.\n- Versions are loosely pinned (`requests==2.32`). That's a reproducibility issue, not a licensing one. You may also want to check for known CVEs before release.\n- I'm not a lawyer. Have counsel review the somelib question before you ship.\n\nIf you'd like, I can look through the repo to see how somelib is used, which would help decide between replacing it and isolating it.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}